  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1225 AM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nThe upper level high will continue to dominate the weather \npattern through Tuesday across the Southern and Central Rockies. \nFor today, we are looking at similar conditions to yesterday. \nMoisture will increase slightly, leading to slightly higher \nrelative humidities and increased cloud cover. Temperatures will \nbe close to yesterday's readings, but the increase in cloud cover \nwill likely knock a couple of degrees off what we saw yesterday. \nThis will put highs in the mid to upper 90s with a few locations \ntopping 100 degrees. Scattered high-based showers and perhaps a \nfew thunderstorms are expected to form again this afternoon. DCAPE\nvalues will be 700-1100 J/kg across the lower elevations (about \n100 J/kg less than yesterday), so we expect gusty outflow winds \nagain. Isolated gusts up to 55 mph will be possible with 30-45 mph\nbeing much more common.\n\nFor Monday and Tuesday, very warm to hot temperatures will \ncontinue with highs in the mid to upper 90s across northeast \nColorado. A mostly sunny day with less smoke could push some \nlocations above 100 degrees. Precipitable water values will be \n90-120 percent of normal both days. The best chance for showers \nand isolated thunderstorms will be over the higher terrain. Very \nlittle to no rainfall is expected across the urban corridor and \neastern plains, but gusty outflow winds will develop again. \n\nThe upper level high begins to weaken and drift southeast \nTuesday. By Wednesday, the high will be centered over Texas with \nsouth to southwest flow aloft. Monsoon moisture will begin to \nincrease leading to better chances for showers and thunderstorms \nacross the area. Temperatures begin to cool with highs expected to\nbe in the mid 80s to lower 90s across northeast Colorado. The \nupper level high will stay southeast of the region, maintaining a \nsouth- southwest flow aloft over Colorado into next weekend. \nMonsoon moisture will continue to stream northward pushing \nprecipitable water values to 130-180 percent of normal. Details on\nwhich day(s) will see the best rainfall are still uncertain and \nwill depend on the timing and placement of weak waves embedded in \nthe flow aloft. Main take away is it will be much cooler beginning\nThursday and into next weekend, with numerous showers and \nthunderstorms expected most days. Locally heavy rainfall leading \nto flooding will be possible, but it's too soon to say where or \nwhen, however, the threat exists.",
